WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.010
thenApply, thenCompose, thenCombine.

2
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.577
Three methods with nearly the same name.

3
00:00:07.577 --> 00:00:08.988
Which one do you pick?

4
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:13.031
And does it even matter, as long as everything is asynchronous?

5
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:18.434
Picture a busy kitchen, and an order for table 4: steak with pepper sauce.

6
00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:23.071
The steak goes on the grill and the sauce on the stove, at the same time.

7
00:00:23.071 --> 00:00:30.820
The ticket carries its own instructions: when the steak and the sauce are both ready, plate them and send them to table 4.

8
00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:33.768
Nobody stands at the grill watching the steak.

9
00:00:33.768 --> 00:00:38.048
A watched steak cooks no faster, and the watcher gets nothing else done.

10
00:00:38.048 --> 00:00:42.744
Whichever cook finishes the last part reads the ticket, and does the next step.

11
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:46.552
The Future from two lessons ago had no room for instructions.

12
00:00:46.552 --> 00:00:48.786
You could only wait on it, or read it.

13
00:00:48.786 --> 00:00:51.667
And a thread that waits in get does nothing else.

14
00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:53.724
That's the cook watching the steak.

15
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:57.990
A CompletableFuture is a ticket you can clip instructions to.

16
00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.182
A ticket plus its instruction is called a stage.

17
00:01:01.182 --> 00:01:03.908
You say: when this is done, do that next.

18
00:01:03.908 --> 00:01:05.437
And then you walk away.

19
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:07.835
Here's the smallest one.

20
00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:09.795
A ticket that already holds 6.

21
00:01:09.795 --> 00:01:13.846
thenApply clips on, times 7, and you get a new ticket, for 42.

22
00:01:13.846 --> 00:01:16.656
Every one of these methods works like that.

23
00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:21.425
It takes a ticket, clips on one instruction, and hands back a new ticket.

24
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:26.001
Which one you use depends on the shape of the instruction.

25
00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:29.468
Plain code, from one value to another, like times 7?

26
00:01:29.468 --> 00:01:30.601
That's thenApply.

27
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:37.623
Now say the next step orders dessert from the pastry station, and the pastry station hands back its own ticket.

28
00:01:37.623 --> 00:01:41.661
With thenApply, you'd get a ticket whose result is another ticket.

29
00:01:41.661 --> 00:01:45.148
A ticket for a ticket, and nobody collects the inner one.

30
00:01:45.148 --> 00:01:50.103
thenCompose flattens the two into one ticket, which is ready when the dessert is.

31
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:55.865
If you know Optional or streams: thenApply is map, and thenCompose is flatMap.

32
00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:57.831
And the order for table 4?

33
00:01:57.831 --> 00:02:01.155
Two tickets, and one instruction that needs both.

34
00:02:01.155 --> 00:02:02.444
That's thenCombine.

35
00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:05.453
Let's try it on the lesson's shop.

36
00:02:05.453 --> 00:02:07.446
Its checkout needs two answers.

37
00:02:07.446 --> 00:02:12.654
A price in euros from one service, and the exchange rate to dollars from another.

38
00:02:12.654 --> 00:02:14.454
Each takes 300 milliseconds.

39
00:02:14.454 --> 00:02:19.791
The price is the steak, the rate is the sauce, and multiplying them is the plating.

40
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:27.155
CombineDemo starts both with supplyAsync, on a pool called io, and joins them with thenCombine.

41
00:02:27.155 --> 00:02:29.537
How long until the total is ready?

42
00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:33.876
A little over 300.

43
00:02:33.876 --> 00:02:39.448
main built the whole pipeline in 36 milliseconds and walked away.

44
00:02:39.448 --> 00:02:45.019
The price arrived at 330, and the rate at 332, on two io threads.

45
00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:49.648
The total, 108 dollars and 42 cents, was ready at 347.

46
00:02:49.648 --> 00:02:54.191
And the multiply ran on whichever cook finished last.

47
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.815
Now a version that looks just as good.

48
00:02:56.815 --> 00:03:00.883
It fetches the rate inside thenCompose, after the price arrives.

49
00:03:00.883 --> 00:03:04.760
It's just as asynchronous, and main still walks away at once.

50
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:05.968
How long this time?

51
00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:10.606
The price at 340.

52
00:03:10.606 --> 00:03:12.337
The rate at 644.

53
00:03:12.337 --> 00:03:14.177
The total at 661.

54
00:03:14.177 --> 00:03:18.180
Twice as long, and nothing warned us.

55
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.645
Work starts when you call supplyAsync, not when you call thenCombine.

56
00:03:22.645 --> 00:03:29.904
In the chain, the rate call sits inside an instruction, and an instruction runs only when the stage before it is done.

57
00:03:29.904 --> 00:03:35.687
In the kitchen, that's putting the sauce on the stove only when the steak comes off the grill.

58
00:03:35.687 --> 00:03:39.440
The steak goes cold, and table 4 starts looking at its watch.

59
00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:43.708
So the point is, asynchronous isn't the same as side by side.

60
00:03:43.708 --> 00:03:46.955
Start everything independent first, then combine.

61
00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:50.209
Here's the checkout in Java and Kotlin.

62
00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:54.315
Start both calls, then clip on one instruction that needs both.

63
00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:56.035
Two guardrails.

64
00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.305
First, pass a pool to supplyAsync.

65
00:03:58.305 --> 00:04:03.446
Without one, the work goes to the common pool, one pool the whole JVM shares.

66
00:04:03.446 --> 00:04:10.591
That's the shared kitchen that everyone borrows and nobody owns, and a few slow calls fill it for everyone.

67
00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.166
Second, a plain step runs on whichever cook finished the stage before it.

68
00:04:16.166 --> 00:04:18.973
So keep plain steps cheap, like a multiply.

69
00:04:18.973 --> 00:04:22.172
For a slow step, name a pool with thenApplyAsync.

70
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:23.838
So, which one?

71
00:04:23.838 --> 00:04:26.316
Pick by the shape of the instruction.

72
00:04:26.316 --> 00:04:27.790
Plain code: thenApply.

73
00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.206
A step that hands back its own ticket: thenCompose.

74
00:04:31.206 --> 00:04:34.689
Two tickets and a step that needs both: thenCombine.

75
00:04:34.689 --> 00:04:38.172
And start everything independent before you combine.

76
00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:39.680
Quick check.

77
00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.152
findAsync returns a ticket for a dish.

78
00:04:42.152 --> 00:04:45.859
cookAsync takes a dish, and returns a ticket for a plate.

79
00:04:45.859 --> 00:04:49.892
What do you get from findAsync, then thenApply with cookAsync?

80
00:04:52.867 --> 00:04:54.352
A ticket for a ticket.

81
00:04:54.352 --> 00:04:58.064
Use thenCompose, and you get one ticket, for the plate.
